### <a name="output-generation"></a>Output Generation

Test run with the following data: 

1. An ArrayList where the elements are [1, 2, 3]
2. An element (x) to be inserted as 4
3. An index (index) to insert x at as 1

Output from the execution is as follows:

```
Value	View Name	Value
3	  $old_arrList_Count	3
4	  $new_arrList_Count	4
4	  $old_arrList_x	4
1	  $old_arrList_index	1
1	  $old_arrList_IndexOf_x	1
1	  $new_arrList_IndexOf_x	1
2	  $old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x	1
3	  $new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x	3
true	  $old_arrList_contains_x	true
true	  $new_arrList_contains_x	true
```
This output tells us the old ArrayList count, the new ArrayList count, the x value, its original index, its position when the ArrayList is searched for the previous index of x, its new position when the ArrayList is searched for the previous index of x, its original LastIndexOf position, and its new LastIndexOf position. Lastly, it shows whether the ArrayLists contain x.

### <a name="test-results"></a>Test Results

The test results show that the ArrayList.Insert method works correctly. The previous ArrayList count was 3, after insertion, the count is 4, as expected. The new ArrayList contains the element (4) as you can see from its position (1) in the list and its LastIndexOf position as 3. The ArrayList does indeed contain the element (4). This clearly indicates that the insert operation was successful. 

Thus, the test provides insight into the operation of the ArrayList's Insert method.

Remember, to make Pex work effectively, we should make our methods as deterministic as possible. This includes the use of deterministic algorithms (i.e., ones that produce consistent results given the same input), avoiding global (shared) state among multiple threads, and always specifying return types for your methods. 

In our case, since the ArrayList's Insert method, as defined by the System.Collections.ArrayList.Insert method in the Sdk under .NET Reflect, takes the responsibility of inserting items into their appropriate position (i.e., without altering the size or order of the ArrayList) this makes it safe to depend on.

For more advanced testing scenarios, or for classes that have side-effects beyond simple method calls (i.e., modifying a database, writing to a file, etc.) we would need to craft a more sophisticated approach using fakes or mocks - tools provided by Microsoft Fakes Framework. These tools can simulate the behavior of your dependencies and isolate your unit tests from any complexities they might have. 

Last note, always remember to run your tests under Pex, to fully test the boundary conditions and exceptions. Happy Testing folks.

