```

In this contract test, we observe the list before and after inserting an item into the list. We then perform a series of assertions to validate the contract:

1. The count of the ArrayList increases by one.
2. The newly inserted item's position is at the specified index.
3. The positions of the new item in IndexOf and LastIndexOf remain the same.
4. When we check Contains, it returns true for the new item.

Note that using Debug.Assert helps us catch early bugs. Assertions are particularly useful when the condition fails, as they cause an exception with the specified error message.

You should add assertion attributes to your unit test. You will be reminded to add them whenever the assertions need to be checked during the test run.

## Testing for Exception Conditions ##

As we want to achieve "all paths green" during the unit testing, it is also important to test exception paths for our functions. Some unit testing tools for this purpose include "Expected exceptions" and "Suppress assertions".

For example:

```csharp
[TestMethod] 
[ExpectedException(typeof(ArgumentOutOfRangeException))] 
public void TestRemove_IndexIsLessThan0() 
{ 
  ArrayList arrList = new ArrayList(); 
  arrList.RemoveAt(-1); 
}
```

This test function `TestRemove_IndexIsLessThan0` tests the boundary condition where an attempt is made to remove an item from an ArrayList that does not contain it. We expect an `ArgumentOutOfRangeException` to be thrown.

To suppress assertions or to prevent the unit testing tool from stopping after an error is found, you need to use special attributes such as `[ExpectedException]` or `[SuppressMessage("Microsoft.Design", "CA1031:DoNotCatchGeneralExceptionTypes")]`.

## Summary ##

In summary, unit testing should be carried out to check whether the code functions correctly and to understand whether the unit of work has handled all possible scenarios (i.e., all paths green). It enhances the reliability of your code by finding out errors and exceptions ahead of time, making it easier to fix. Additionally, it supports other team members to understand the functionality of the code. This practice should be adopted throughout the Software Development Life Cycle.

Note: Keep in mind, since you are dealing with an array based list, you have to ensure that your test data are selected carefully as the test data should cover all the scenarios for the boundary testing.
  
Hope these tips and best practices are helpful for you. If you have any other concerns or questions, feel free to ask.   

